Switch (deko3d): a Tint GLSL option to keep gl_Position.y as WebGPU wrote it

Tint's GLSL writer negates gl_Position.y in every vertex shader, for GL's lower-left framebuffer
origin (raise/shader_io.cc). The deko3d renderer (docs/DEKO3D_MIGRATION_PLAN.md section 3.1) creates
its device with DkDeviceFlags_OriginUpperLeft | DepthZeroToOne, whose clip space is WebGPU's (y up,
row 0 at the top, z in [0, w]), so its shaders must write y unchanged.
Options::disable_position_y_negation (default false: Dawn's GL backend never sets it, the GL NRO's
shaders are unchanged) skips the negation, next to depth_zero_to_one of
dawn-switch-gl-clip-control.patch, on top of which it applies.

Applied by native/tools/dksh_cache (the offline DKSH builder) to its copy of the Dawn source, and
later by the deko3d NRO's build. Not applied by dawn.cmake: a new Options field changes the key of
Dawn's GLSL blob cache, which would make the GL NRO's program cache cold once for nothing.

--- a/src/tint/lang/glsl/writer/common/options.h
+++ b/src/tint/lang/glsl/writer/common/options.h
@@ -223,6 +223,11 @@
     /// as WebGPU does, so gl_Position.z is written unchanged instead of remapped to GL's [-w, w]
     /// range by 2 * z - w (which rounds z to about 2^-24 of w).
     bool depth_zero_to_one = false;
+
+    /// Switch (deko3d renderer): write gl_Position.y unchanged instead of negating it. deko3d with
+    /// DkDeviceFlags_OriginUpperLeft has WebGPU's clip space (y up, framebuffer row 0 at the top),
+    /// so the flip GL's lower-left origin needs is wrong there. Dawn's GL backend never sets it.
+    bool disable_position_y_negation = false;
 
     /// Vertex inputs to perform BGRA swizzle on.
